# K5 - The write matrix and privacy. Kata sandbox sketch (plan section 3.6).
# [K5-nn] sketch IDs are recognised by validate_architecture / trace and ignored by the
# CI adl-coverage gate. Never [TR-*]; promotion is decided only at K12 (architecture/REVIEW.md).
DESCRIPTION K5 - which file may write which persisted artifact, and nothing reaches the org remote unfiltered
CATEGORY Data
NAMESPACE TrK5NS
DEFINE SYSTEM Total Recall K5 Sandbox AS trk5
    DEFINE DATABASE Index AS index
    DEFINE DATABASE Vector Store AS vectordb
    DEFINE COMPONENT Memory Files AS memfiles
    DEFINE COMPONENT Org Push AS orgpush

# Course pp. 33-38: access is described with assertions on who may touch which database, not by
# nesting. The vault has five persisted artifacts with a declared writer set each (index.json and
# invertedIndex.json: persistence; .index-cache.txt: the cache builder, TR-W8; vectors.db: the vector
# store). Memory .md files and the journal are written by raw filesystem calls, so the same rule is
# an inventory: any production file with a raw write sink must be named.
ASSERT(Index: each persisted artifact is written only by its declared writer files, and every production file with a raw filesystem write is on the writer inventory) # [K5-01]

# TR-D3 says the org push is filtered. The matrix reading: every file in the repo that can run a
# git push is itself wired to the privacy filter, in any language (TS, mjs, shell).
ASSERT(Org Push: every file that can run a git push imports the privacy filter, and the only such file is sync-org-memory.mjs) # [K5-02]

# Not re-asserted, answered with existing evidence (answers.md): vault routing (TR-D1, TR-D2),
# the filter fails closed (TR-D3), atomic writes (TR-W1), one cache writer (TR-W8).
